“To the b&#8212;h that&#8217;s booing, you didn&#8217;t have to come here,” Blige said to the fan — as captured by TMZ — in the midst of her show at Sin City&#8217;s Dolby Live at Park MGM. &#8220;I hope someone sees you and politely removes you.&#8221;

The R&B icon continued to slam the heckler and apologized to the crowd for the disruption, blaming her Yonkers roots for clapping back and putting an end to the nonsense.

&#8220;The f&#8211;k you spend your money for, idiot?&#8221; she piled on. &#8220;I&#8217;m sorry. Sometimes I have to go in because that&#8217;s where I&#8217;m from.&#8221;

Mary J. Blige got back to performing as usual and continued the show like nothing took place. Shortly after, the 55-year-old brought out Wyclef Jean as a special guest to perform their top 40 Billboard Hot 100 hit &#8220;911.&#8221;

Blige will return to the stage on Friday (Oct. 30) with a second weekend show on tap for Halloween at Dolby Live, which is the Grammy-winning singer&#8217;s last residency date of 2026. The My Life, My Story residency will conclude in 2027 with five dates in February.

&#8220;This residency felt like divine timing,&#8221; the Rock and Roll Hall of Famer said in a statement in September when the ending of the residency was revealed. &#8220;I’ve seen so many of you in Vegas and we still have 10 dates left. I feel even more grateful and deeply inspired after every show. Grab your girlfriends, your boots, and I’ll see you in Vegas in October and February.&#8221;

Blige&#8217;s last album,  Gratitude , arrived in November 2024. It marked her first studio LP not to make the Billboard 200 .
